Consider this the pre-final announcement.  The attached images [in the 
followup posts[ is what the Commands directory looks like if you follow the 
serving suggestion in the forthcoming manual [which is now even more 
obsolete because this was changed so much!]  It is clearly getting close 
and your feedback is solicited. and related subordinate windows [which 
didn't exist before].

Still not changed are my "choice" for icons.  I can say they are consistent 
to each other, but leave a lot to be desired in terms of replacing them.  
I'm not an icon design guy, and have no "eye" for such things other than I 
know what I like when I see it.  So, any of you who can step up to the 
plate on this, your assistance is highly welcome.  In any case, these 
attached Windows gets you pretty much what it will look like. [Note: I've 
been using the package as it evolves.  No "rules" say you have to do it 
this way, but as the most experienced and heavy user of it, I wouldn't 
recommend changing it just to be different.  From a functionality 
standpoint, I think this is as good as it gets.  I generally use details 
view for the majority of things, but not in this case.

There is a subtlety going on here that only Windows installation experts 
would know about.

In all versions of Windows prior to Windows 7, there is a feature build 
into EVERY window to make presentation of icons to be as easy as it is on 
the desktop.  That said, no one is suggesting you pervert your Windows 
system to be a "PEPS=only" device.  AS such, we need to have free reign 
over where icons can be grouped, and not forced by the fact that, as 
released Windows 7 and up force name order and auto-arrange that just 
places them all by name in order from left to right and top to bottom so 
there is maximum white space on the bottom.  I have tried this and it makes 
it VERY tedious to use.  [Almost would rather use Details view if those 
were the only choices!].

But fortunately, in Windows XP and Windows Vista, you can just uncheck 
Auto-Arrange and also optionally Align to Grid [which is actually useful, 
that meaning the latter WITHOUT the former] and it's an available setting.

But a lot of internal Microsoft "politics" happened with the release of Win 
7 .[If you want a name, it's Balmer, whom IMHO did the best thing he ever 
did at that point - quit MS to run a football team.

Throughout the history of Windows, programmers "conformed" to management 
edicts, or at least they pretended to.  A Windows hacker could figure out 
the right thing to do, so if you nosed around, you find what you SHOULD 
have had [easier].  So, when some empty suit says we "improved" it by 
removing something, and we all know that is generally laughable, the good 
news is that with some effort, people will dig into how to get it back 
anyway.  I spent a lot of time reading MS-sanctioned things online claiming 
what was done is "impossible"  and it is "because we removed that old 
feature I think it's better to not have any more" drivel.  This is a screen 
shot from Windows 7, and proof positive they are all fools [as many already 
suspected].

What IT true is that to implement this, you will lose ALL of your current 
settings ONCE.  Put them all back as you see tit.  Since you won't have to 
disrupt it again, you can use the "new" features for all of your Windows 
icon arrangement needs, not just PEPS, and having seen this serving 
suggestion might be food for thought about incorporating it elsewhere, etc. 
[Bear in mind that we who used XP just naturally did a lot of that if we 
pondered the problem.  And of course, MS are hypocrites  because you can do 
it on the DESKTOP [but ONLY the desktoip].

There is also a caveat.  Anything that disrupts things such as changing 
screen resolution will destroy the settings, which can always be put back.  
when you install PEPS, follow the manual and perform the arrangement 
yourself.  You may have to do it again if something "happens" unexpectedly 
[such as changing a monitor or screen resolution for whatever reason.  
Thus, it works, but it is "fragile".  And with all of my looking, I never 
found a restore windows positions in OTHER THAN the desktop, etc. [Hmm, 
maybe if you dedicate your machine to PEPS and put them on the actual 
desktop is starting to sound a bit more attractive!].

In any case, once it is applied, things start to work the way we took for 
granted in XP and Vista.

I am not done with the patch, in part because it needs to be tested on 
8/8.1 and 10.  Where I obtained it from indicated that this is a subset of 
another package for 8, but that the differences are unrelated to what is 
needed here, e.g., what I will release will definitely run on 7 and 8.  
What is unknown is what Win10 will do, so that will take some time; I have 
to put up both 8 and 10 to be sure.

Other than this one item [which is itself on the Setup window] all else 
seems to work fine, thus this posting.

I will follow up with a post briefly describing what each does, but I've 
learned that in Google Groups,it's best to keep things short.

cjl

